<h2>Find the Perimeter: some and all side lengths</h2>
<p>Original four-page student activities: <a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/16rg19q0jfil1ylgznbqnq11pe8s" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">View original 2021 student source diagrams</a>. The images remain at the publisher, not copied into this site.</p>
<p>Warm-up: explain why 90÷9=10, 81÷9=9, 45÷9=5 and 54÷9=6. Partner notice-and-wonder compares a 5 by 3 dot-grid rectangle with one labelled 5 and 3.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Original pupil question</th><th>Correct perimeter</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>8.1 #1 triangle</th><td>30 cm</td><td>10+8+12</td></tr>
<tr><th>8.1 #2 rectangle</th><td>80 inches</td><td>2(16+24)</td></tr>
<tr><th>8.1 #3 concave pentagon</th><td>36 cm</td><td>13+5+5+11+2</td></tr>
<tr><th>8.1 #4 hexagon</th><td>32 feet</td><td>2(8)+4(4)</td></tr>
<tr><th>8.1 #5 equal-sided quadrilateral</th><td>36 cm</td><td>4(9)</td></tr>
<tr><th>8.2 #1 rectangle missing opposite sides</th><td>60 cm</td><td>2(21+9)</td></tr>
<tr><th>8.2 #2 right-angled 12-gon</th><td>640 unspecified units</td><td>11(40)+5(40)</td></tr>
<tr><th>8.2 #3 equal-sided octagon</th><td>240 inches</td><td>8(30)</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Never assume the stepped shape's missing long side is 40: it spans five short lengths. A number alone is not proof of the missing-side drawing or partner explanation. Teacher reviews source diagrams and gallery-walk reasoning.</p>
